DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Philosophy Competition: First Edition
DESCRIPTION:The AI Philosophy Competition\, organised by philosophers Zacha
 ry Goodsell and Elliott Thornley and funded by Forethought Research\, aims
  to gauge the philosophical capabilities of AI in a way transparent to the
  philosophical community.\n\nOrganisation: Zachary Goodsell and Elliott Th
 ornley\, funded by Forethought Research\nLocation: Online submission via O
 penReview (preferred) or Google Forms\; open internationally.\nAward: USD 
 3\,000 first prize\, USD 2\,000 second\, USD 1\,000 third\, plus a USD 5\,
 000 pool for creative methodology distributed at the judges' discretion (U
 SD 11\,000 total). No entry fee.\nDuration: Finalists announced early Dece
 mber 2026\; results announced end of January 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.za
 charygoodsell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.zacharygoodsell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online submission via OpenReview (preferred) or Google Forms\; ope
 n internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lukas-work-in-progress-awards-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T160830Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261203
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261204
SUMMARY:Deadline: J. Anthony Lukas Work-in-Progress Awards 2027
DESCRIPTION:The J. Anthony Lukas Work-in-Progress Awards\, given jointly by
  Columbia Journalism School and the Nieman Foundation at Harvard\, are ope
 n for 2027 submissions. Two annual awards of USD 25\,000 each help writers
  complete a book-length work of narrative nonfiction on a topic of America
 n political or social concern\; books on science\, technology and media ac
 countability have won in past years.\n\nOrganisation: Columbia Journalism 
 School and the Nieman Foundation\nLocation: USA. Requires a fully executed
  contract with a US-based publisher.\nAward: Two awards of USD 25\,000 eac
 h. No entry fee for the Work-in-Progress awards.\n\nMore: https://columbia
 journalismawards.submittable.com/submit?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://columbiajournalismawards.submittable.com/submit?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:USA. Requires a fully executed contract with a US-based publisher.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cove-park-awarded-residencies-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T160830Z
DTSTART:20261211T163000Z
DTEND:20261211T170000Z
SUMMARY:Deadline 17:00 GMT: Cove Park Awarded Residencies 2027-2028 (Scotla
 nd\, UK\, Central and South America and diasporas)
DESCRIPTION:Closes 11 Dec 2026 at 17:00 GMT.\n\nCove Park's biennial call f
 or fully funded Awarded Residencies at its 50-acre rural residency site in
  Argyll\, on the west coast of Scotland\, programmed between May 2027 and 
 December 2028.\n\nOrganisation: Cove Park\nLocation: Cove Park\, Peaton Hi
 ll\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, private self-catering accommo
 dation and studio). Open to people based in the UK\, Central or South Amer
 ica\, Central and South American diaspora artists based in Europe or North
  America\, and Scotland-born artists living anywhere. Students in formal e
 ducation are not eligible.\nAward: Fully funded: GBP 775 per week fee (VAT
  inclusive\, freelance)\, travel allowance (Scotland GBP 65 to 200\, rest 
 of UK up to GBP 250\, Europe up to GBP 400\, other international up to GBP
  1\,250)\, materials up to GBP 150 for studio-based practitioners\, privat
 e accommodation and studio\, visa and access support. Application Access B
 ursary available (request by 30 November 2026). No application fee.\nDurat
 ion: 2 to 12 weeks (average 4)\, between May 2027 and December 2028\n\nMor
 e: https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cove Park\, Peaton Hill\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, 
 private self-catering accommodation and studio). Open to people based in t
 he UK\, Central or South America\, Central and South American diaspora art
 ists based in Europe or North America\, and Scotland-born artists living a
 nywhere. Students in formal education are not eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
